window.__labLoaded({"id":"9.1","title":"Study Design: How the Data Were Collected Decides What You Can Claim","goal":"Simulate 200 people with a hidden fitness level and see that a coin-flip assignment balances it between two groups while self-selection does not, which biases the estimated effect.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Create people with a hidden trait","text":"We invent 200 people. Each has a fitness score that we, as researchers, would not normally see. The outcome (say, a health score) depends on fitness and on a program that truly adds 5 points. A seed makes the simulation repeatable.","code":"import numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nn = 200\ntrue_effect = 5\n\nfitness = rng.normal(50, 10, n)\nnoise = rng.normal(0, 5, n)\nbase_outcome = 40 + 0.5 * fitness + noise\n\nprint(f'people = {n}, mean fitness = {fitness.mean():.1f}')","out":"people = 200, mean fitness = 49.7","figs":[]},{"title":"Random assignment: a coin decides","text":"Each person is put in the program or the comparison group by a coin flip, so fitness plays no part. With enough people, the two groups come out alike on fitness, and on every other trait we did not even measure.","code":"random_group = rng.random(n) < 0.5\n\nprint('RANDOM ASSIGNMENT')\nprint(f'  program    : n = {random_group.sum()}, mean fitness = {fitness[random_group].mean():.1f}')\nprint(f'  comparison : n = {(~random_group).sum()}, mean fitness = {fitness[~random_group].mean():.1f}')","out":"RANDOM ASSIGNMENT\n  program    : n = 95, mean fitness = 49.0\n  comparison : n = 105, mean fitness = 50.3","figs":[]},{"title":"Self-selection: people choose","text":"Now fitter people are more likely to sign up (a logistic curve turns fitness into a chance of choosing the program). The groups now differ on fitness before the program has done anything.","code":"chance = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(fitness - 50) / 5))\nchose = rng.random(n) < chance\n\nprint('SELF-SELECTION')\nprint(f'  program    : n = {chose.sum()}, mean fitness = {fitness[chose].mean():.1f}')\nprint(f'  comparison : n = {(~chose).sum()}, mean fitness = {fitness[~chose].mean():.1f}')","out":"SELF-SELECTION\n  program    : n = 98, mean fitness = 54.6\n  comparison : n = 102, mean fitness = 45.0","figs":[]},{"title":"Compare the estimated effects","text":"The program adds exactly 5 points to whoever gets it. Randomised groups should estimate about 5. In the self-selected groups, the program group was fitter to begin with, so the simple difference over-claims.","code":"for label, group in [('random', random_group), ('self-selected', chose)]:\n    outcome = base_outcome + true_effect * group\n    diff = outcome[group].mean() - outcome[~group].mean()\n    print(f'{label:14s} estimated effect = {diff:5.1f}   (true effect = {true_effect})')","out":"random         estimated effect =   4.5   (true effect = 5)\nself-selected  estimated effect =  10.7   (true effect = 5)","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"set.seed(42)\nn <- 200\nfitness <- rnorm(n, 50, 10)\nbase <- 40 + 0.5 * fitness + rnorm(n, 0, 5)\nrandom <- runif(n) < 0.5\nchose <- runif(n) < plogis((fitness - 50) / 5)\ntapply(fitness, random, mean)\ntapply(fitness, chose, mean)\nmean((base + 5 * random)[random]) - mean((base + 5 * random)[!random])\nmean((base + 5 * chose)[chose]) - mean((base + 5 * chose)[!chose])","expect":"Random groups have almost the same mean fitness (a gap of a point or two at most) and the estimated effect is close to 5. The self-selected program group has clearly higher mean fitness (about 8 to 10 points) and the estimated effect is far above 5, around 10. Exact numbers differ from Python.","note":"R uses a different random number generator than Python, so the simulated numbers are close in spirit but not identical. The pattern (balanced when random, unbalanced when chosen) is the same."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (with Random Number functions), then Analyze > Compare Means > Means","syntax":"SET SEED=42.\nINPUT PROGRAM.\nLOOP #i = 1 TO 200.\nEND CASE.\nEND LOOP.\nEND FILE.\nEND INPUT PROGRAM.\nCOMPUTE fitness = RV.NORMAL(50,10).\nCOMPUTE random = (RV.UNIFORM(0,1) < 0.5).\nCOMPUTE chose = (RV.UNIFORM(0,1) < 1/(1+EXP(-(fitness-50)/5))).\nMEANS TABLES=fitness BY random /CELLS=MEAN COUNT.\nMEANS TABLES=fitness BY chose /CELLS=MEAN COUNT.","note":"SPSS draws different random numbers from Python, so simulated numbers are close but not identical. Compare the mean fitness of the two groups in each MEANS table: nearly equal for random, clearly different for chose. The estimated-effect step is left to Python, R and Stata."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset seed 42\nset obs 200\ngenerate fitness = rnormal(50, 10)\ngenerate base = 40 + 0.5 * fitness + rnormal(0, 5)\ngenerate random = runiform() < 0.5\ngenerate chose = runiform() < invlogit((fitness - 50) / 5)\ntabstat fitness, by(random) statistics(mean n)\ntabstat fitness, by(chose) statistics(mean n)\ngenerate outcome_r = base + 5 * random\ngenerate outcome_c = base + 5 * chose\nttest outcome_r, by(random)\nttest outcome_c, by(chose)","note":"Stata draws different random numbers from Python, so simulated numbers are close but not identical. ttest shows mean(0) - mean(1), so the program effect appears with a negative sign (about -5 when random, a larger negative number when chosen)."},"try":["Change the sample size to 2000 and predict what happens to the gap in mean fitness between the random groups.","Make the choice only weakly tied to fitness by dividing (fitness - 50) by 20 instead of 5. Predict how the self-selected estimate moves relative to the true effect of 5."]});
